---
import BlogPostLayout from '../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro';

const contentHtml = `
<p><strong>Kiln 1.1.8 is a big release.</strong> Kiln now stops a print that physically can&rsquo;t succeed before it wastes filament, answers whether a printed part will survive the liquid you have in mind, and ships a new page where you can check your printer is supported in seconds. On the paid tiers: the right glue for joining printed parts (with prep and cure times from the manufacturer&rsquo;s own sheets), materials imported straight from a datasheet, and factory-grade assembly instructions in seven languages.</p>

<h2>Doomed prints get stopped before they start</h2>

<p>Some prints were never going to work: the part is bigger than the printer&rsquo;s build area (the space it can physically print inside), or the material needs more heat than the printer&rsquo;s hotend can deliver. Kiln now catches both before any plastic moves, free for everyone. If rotating the part would make it fit, Kiln rotates it. If it isn&rsquo;t sure, it never blocks you, and there&rsquo;s a one-command override for when you&rsquo;re deliberately slicing for a different printer. Upgrade to Pro tier or higher and Kiln goes from &ldquo;it won&rsquo;t fit&rdquo; to &ldquo;here&rsquo;s how to make it&rdquo;: your printer&rsquo;s true usable area (often a little smaller than the brochure number), the cleanest way to split an oversize part into pieces that fit, and a material to switch to when the one you picked won&rsquo;t melt.</p>

<h2>Check if Kiln supports your printer, instantly</h2>

<p>There&rsquo;s a new <a href="https://kiln3d.com/printers">Supported Printers</a> page listing every model Kiln ships a tuned profile for: Bambu Lab, Creality, Prusa, Elegoo, Sovol, Voron, AnkerMake, and more, with a live filter so you find your exact machine in seconds. Running something else? Any printer that speaks Klipper, Marlin, Bambu, Prusa Link, or OctoPrint works too, with safe generic settings. This release also adds a tuned profile for the Sovol SV07 Plus and corrects the standard SV07&rsquo;s build volume.</p>

<h2>Will this part survive gasoline? Vinegar? A summer in the sun?</h2>

<p>Printed parts live rough lives: fuel cans, cleaning caddies, garden fixtures. Kiln now answers for the exact liquid or exposure you have in mind, and it&rsquo;s honest that a printed part takes chemicals harder than solid plastic does. The warnings are always free: if it&rsquo;s a bad idea, Kiln says so. The go-aheads are paid: everyday exposures (cleaners, oils, sunlight, weather) are Kiln Business, and the answers someone could get hurt by (fuels, automotive fluids, food contact, harsh chemicals) are Enterprise.</p>

<h2>The right glue, and how to use it <em>(Kiln Pro)</em></h2>

<p>Joining two printed parts with &ldquo;some superglue&rdquo; is how joints fail. Upgrade to Pro tier or higher and Kiln recommends the right adhesive for your two plastics, with the surface prep and the full cure schedule (working time, clamp time, full cure), every number cited to the manufacturer&rsquo;s own datasheet. It&rsquo;s honest about the hard-to-bond plastics and the primer they need, and your assembly instructions name the specific glue instead of a vague &ldquo;use adhesive.&rdquo; Log how a joint held up and Kiln learns: glues that actually hold float up the ranking, clearly marked as field experience, never replacing the cited specs. Everyone on the free tier still gets the safety warnings and a pointer to the right tool.</p>

<h2>Deeper know-how for budget printers <em>(Kiln Pro)</em></h2>

<p>The model-specific knowledge Kiln brings to a print (quirks, common failure modes, calibration tips) now runs as deep for budget and mid-range machines as it does for the flagships.</p>

<h2>Bring your own materials <em>(Business)</em></h2>

<p>Got a filament Kiln doesn&rsquo;t know? Import the manufacturer&rsquo;s datasheet and Kiln turns it into a material it can design with, right alongside the built-in catalog. Every value is checked against the sheet itself: anything the sheet doesn&rsquo;t say stays unknown rather than guessed. Materials you import work across your whole account, not just the machine you imported them on.</p>

<h2>Your agent gets the whole story in one call <em>(Kiln Pro)</em></h2>

<p>Ask your agent about a design and it used to gather the story piece by piece. Part Passport hands it everything at once: versions, materials, assembly manual, print outcomes, releases. Less back-and-forth, faster answers.</p>

<h2>Design changes your team can actually govern <em>(Business)</em></h2>

<p>Put an approval gate on a protected design and a change can&rsquo;t merge until the required people have reviewed it. Your own approval doesn&rsquo;t count, an open &ldquo;request changes&rdquo; holds the line, and the rule covers every way a design can move, so review can&rsquo;t be sidestepped. Enterprise adds two more: require a specific reviewer for a specific part of a design (the structural engineer on anything load-bearing), and releases signed by a named, authorized person, for regulated design records.</p>

<h2>Factory instructions that travel <em>(Enterprise)</em></h2>

<p>Enterprise assembly instructions can now generate as controlled, shop-floor work instructions: a procurable parts list, per-step torque and pass/fail checks, sign-offs before the steps you can&rsquo;t undo. New in 1.1.8, they generate end to end in English, German, French, Spanish, Italian, Chinese, or Japanese, so a line anywhere in the world builds the same kit from the same source in its own language. Tell Kiln what went wrong on a build (a stripped screw, a joint that let go) and the next guide for that design warns you on that exact step. Opt in, and what builders log about joints is pooled anonymously, by joint type and material only, so a guide can flag a joint that&rsquo;s bitten others and name the fix that held. Kiln can also flag the bought parts in a design that are going end-of-life or single-source, before you commit to a parts list you can&rsquo;t buy in a year.</p>

<h2>Straighter answers about strength</h2>

<p>When you ask if a printed part can hold a load, Kiln now says up front that it&rsquo;s giving you a quick estimate, not a test of your real part, and it names the things you should never trust an estimate for without an engineer: overhead loads, anything a person climbs on, child or vehicle parts. Borderline geometry shows up as a note instead of going quiet. Free flags what&rsquo;s risky; Kiln Pro tunes the analysis to your printer.</p>

<h2>Smaller fixes that add up</h2>

<p>A few more: fleet status now shows each printer&rsquo;s reported nozzle size and material, so you can route abrasive filaments to the right machine. Roll your own rolling tray, with your art on the work surface and your brand or a QR code underneath <em>(Kiln Pro)</em>. Before a print that&rsquo;s meant to touch food, Kiln checks your setup is food-safe and asks you to confirm once if it can&rsquo;t be sure <em>(Kiln Pro)</em>. And cross-organization design moves got stricter about who&rsquo;s allowed to make them.</p>

<p>Full release notes: <a href="https://github.com/codeofaxel/Kiln/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md">CHANGELOG.md</a>.</p>

<h2>Upgrading</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Already use Kiln?</strong> Run <code>pip install --upgrade kiln3d</code> to move to 1.1.8.</li>
  <li><strong>On a paid tier?</strong> Upgrade to 1.1.8 too, so the chemical, adhesive, and factory features line up.</li>
  <li><strong>New here?</strong> Run <code>pip install kiln3d</code>, then follow the <a href="/install">install guide</a> to connect Kiln to your AI assistant: Claude Desktop, Claude Code, Codex, or any other compatible AI client.</li>
</ul>

<p style="text-align: center; margin-top: 40px;"><a href="/install" class="btn">Get Kiln &rarr;</a></p>
`;
